# text = It is not to be wondered at that poor Twemlow should decline to inflict a letter on his noble cousin ( who has gout in the temper ), inasmuch as his noble cousin , who allows him a small annuity on which he lives , takes it out of him , as the phrase goes , in extreme severity ; putting him , when he visits at Snigsworthy Park , under a kind of martial law ; ordaining that he shall hang his hat on a particular peg , sit on a particular chair , talk on particular subjects to particular people , and perform particular exercises : such as sounding the praises of the Family Varnish ( not to say Pictures ), and abstaining from the choicest of the Family Wines unless expressly invited to partake says Twemlow Veneering blesses him again says Twemlow , in a rising hurry of spirits , ' to the club ;-- let us see now ; what o'clock is it says Twemlow Veneering feels that his friends are rallying round him , and says replies Twemlow says Veneering cries Twemlow , polite little gentleman that he is remarks Veneering rejoins Twemlow , feelingly Veneering again blesses him , plunges down stairs , rushes into his Hansom , and directs the driver to be up and at the British Public , and to charge into the City .
